\paragraph{Reframing the intervention target.} Any gradient-level strategy that targets the reward head alone (reward prediction regularization, reward-conditioned value clipping, or reward-only SGS) targets the largest pathway but not the informationally critical one.  The failure of exp\_016e (NVE $10.42\times$) despite detaching the dominant gradient source confirms this.  Full-pathway SGS (exp\_018) detaches $e_{\Delta t}$ from all non-KL gradients, retaining only the KL dynamics contribution (34--40\%).  However, this intervention yields mixed results: seed~1 avoids collapse (eval 448.3) while seed~2 collapses (eval 40.8). Removing the observation decoder gradient channel is thus necessary but not sufficient. Figure~\ref{fig:civo_decomposition} decomposes evaluation returns across conditions.

\begin{figure}[ht]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.75\columnwidth]{figures/fig2_civo_decomposition.pdf}
\caption{CIVO decomposition: peak and final (105K step) evaluation returns on Walker Walk across four conditions. \textbf{Baseline} (001): vanilla DreamerV3. \textbf{Trained dt-emb} (008d)\protect\footnotemark: learned $d{=}32$ temporal embedding with multi-rate data. \textbf{Full-SGS} (s1, s2): trained embedding with all non-KL gradients detached from $e_{\dt}$. Trained $\dt$-embedding degrades peak eval by $73\%$ and final eval by $86\%$. Full-SGS seed~1 partially recovers ($-51\%$ final) but seed~2 collapses ($-96\%$ final). Recovery rate $= 50\%$.}
\label{fig:civo_decomposition}
\end{figure}
\footnotetext{Experiment 008d terminated at 55K steps; the 105K data point uses continuation run 009e under identical configuration.}

