\subsection{F3: Unexposed Temporal Embedding Suppresses Value Drift}

\subsection{Online CIVO Detection}
\label{sec:detector}

The $\nve$ diagnostic requires a reference baseline unavailable during deployment. We propose a lightweight rule-based detector that monitors the value-to-evaluation ratio $r_t = \bar{V}_t / \max(\bar{E}_t, \varepsilon)$ and fires when four conditions hold simultaneously: magnitude ($r_t \geq 0.49$), positive velocity ($\Delta r_t > 0.1$), acceleration ($\Delta r_t > \Delta r_{t-1}$), and evaluation decline ($\geq 2$ of 3 recent checkpoints declining). CIVO's positive feedback loop produces superlinear growth in $r_t$, which the acceleration condition captures as a causal signature that distinguishes pathological divergence from normal value learning. Applied retrospectively to 14 runs across three environments and two simulators (DMC Walker Walk, DMC Cheetah Run, ManiSkill3 PickCube-v1), the detector achieves TPR $= 5/5$, FPR $= 2/9$ (precision 71\%, F$_1 = 0.83$). Two of five true positives are predictive (alert before performance degradation at 45K steps). The PickCube-v1 case (exp\_019b) validates specificity in a sparse-reward manipulation domain: the V/E ratio spikes transiently but the acceleration condition never co-occurs with evaluation decline, producing a correct TN. Algorithm details, threshold sensitivity analysis, per-run validation results, and detector limitations are in Appendix~\ref{sec:detector_appendix}.

