\paragraph{F6: $\dt{=}1$ imagination does not prevent all collapse.}
Exp\_008e\_v3 restricts imagination to $\dt{=}1$ while retaining the trained embedding. Two of four seeds are healthy (s1: eval 869.9, $\nve = 0.94\times$; s4: eval 899.3, $\nve = 0.99\times$), while two collapse (50\%) via qualitatively distinct mechanisms (Figure~\ref{fig:multiple_pathways}). Seed~3 (Pathway~A) collapses via the CIVO mechanism with entropy decrease ($\nve = 4.02\times$, eval 92.7). Seed~2 (Pathway~B) collapses with \emph{increasing} entropy ($-5.21 \rightarrow -3.83$, $\nve = 3.78\times$, eval 137.0) while values remain stable. The bimodal outcome, with no intermediate states, reveals multiple independent vulnerability mechanisms. Pathway~B (008e\_v3\_s2) produces the sole non-CIVO false positive in the NVE classification.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.65\columnwidth]{figures/paper/fig6_multiple_pathways.pdf}
\caption{Contrasting collapse dynamics. \textbf{Left:} 008d (CIVO), value explosion with entropy decrease. \textbf{Right:} 008e\_v3 s2 (non-CIVO), evaluation crash with entropy increase.}
\label{fig:multiple_pathways}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{F7: Full-pathway gradient stopping shows mixed results.}
Exp\_018 applies Selective Gradient Stopping (SGS) by detaching all non-KL gradients from $e_{\dt}$; this blocks the observation decoder pathway identified in F2 as the primary CIVO conduit. Figure~\ref{fig:civo_decomposition} decomposes evaluation returns across conditions. Trained $\dt$-embedding alone produces $-73\%$ peak and $-86\%$ final eval degradation relative to the baseline. Full-SGS seed~1 avoids collapse (eval \sgseval{}, $-51\%$ final relative to baseline) while seed~2 collapses (eval 40.8, $-96\%$); the overall recovery rate is 50\%. SGS reduces but does not eliminate CIVO risk: the gradient pathway it blocks is necessary but not sufficient for reliable elimination. Reward-only SGS (016e) fails entirely (NVE $10.42\times$). Cross-environment validation on Cheetah Run (010b, 2 seeds) confirms CIVO generalization: both seeds collapse (peak NVE $8.27\times$ and $108.52\times$, final eval 51.7 and 0.83 at 105K). Full-SGS on Cheetah Run enables recovery from transient CIVO onset (NVE $9.94\times$ at 45K, eval 4.6) to functional performance (eval 221.5 at 105K), in contrast to permanent collapse without SGS (010b: eval 51.7, 0.83 across 2 seeds). Combined with Walker results (50\% recovery rate), SGS is necessary but not sufficient for CIVO elimination. Removing the embedding on Cheetah eliminates CIVO entirely (024b, 2 seeds, NVE $< 0.2\times$, eval 96.5/77.3), replicating the Walker causal control (008f) in a second environment.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.75\columnwidth]{figures/fig2_civo_decomposition.pdf}
\caption{CIVO decomposition: peak and final (105K step) evaluation returns on Walker Walk (DMC) across four conditions. \textbf{Baseline} (001): vanilla DreamerV3, no temporal embedding. \textbf{Trained dt-emb} (008d)\protect\footnotemark: DreamerV3 with a learned $d{=}32$ temporal embedding exposed to multi-rate data. \textbf{Full-SGS} (s1, s2): trained embedding with Selective Gradient Stopping that detaches all non-KL gradients from $e_{\dt}$, two seeds. Trained $\dt$-embedding degrades peak eval by $73\%$ and final eval by $86\%$ relative to the baseline. Full-SGS seed~1 partially recovers ($-51\%$ final, no collapse) but seed~2 collapses ($-96\%$ final, marked ``collapsed''), SGS enables recovery in some seeds but does not reliably prevent CIVO (recovery rate $= 50\%$). All percentages relative to Baseline 001.}
\label{fig:civo_decomposition}
\end{figure}
\footnotetext{Experiment 008d terminated at 55K steps; the 105K data point uses continuation run 009e under identical configuration.}

