\paragraph{Temporal Abstraction in World-Model RL.}
The options framework \citep{sutton1999options} and model-free action-repetition methods \citep{sharma2017figar, biedenkapp2021temporl, lakshminarayanan2017dynamic} achieve temporal abstraction by training values on real transitions. In world-model RL, the Dreamer family \citep{hafner2020dreamerv1, hafner2021dreamerv2, hafner2023dreamerv3} and transformer-based variants \citep{micheli2023iris, hafner2025dreamerv4} train policies in imagination at fixed $\dt{=}1$. Hierarchical approaches \citep{gumbsch2024thick, mattes2024hieros, li2025lsimagine} introduce temporal abstraction via separate dynamics per scale, but none conditions a flat dynamics model on $\dt$ for imagination-based policy training. TAWM \citep{nhu2025tawm} conditions a world model on $\dt$ within TD-MPC2 \citep{hansen2024tdmpc2} and achieves consistent gains; the critical distinction is that MPC re-plans from real states, so model errors do not accumulate into a critic (\S\ref{sec:discussion}).

\paragraph{Value Overestimation.}
Maximization bias \citep{thrun1993issues} has been addressed by Double Q-learning \citep{vanhasselt2016double}, TD3 \citep{fujimoto2018td3}, SAC \citep{haarnoja2018sac}, and REDQ \citep{chen2021redq}. In model-based settings, MBPO \citep{janner2019mbpo} bounds model use via short rollouts and MOPO \citep{yu2020mopo} penalizes rewards by model uncertainty. \citet{lu2018nondelusional} identify delusional bias from policy-class constraints; \citet{nauman2024overestimation} show that overestimation and plasticity loss are coupled. CIVO is mechanistically distinct: the amplifier is the world model's co-adaptation with a learned temporal embedding, producing systematic reward-prediction bias at $\dt > 1$ (\S\ref{sec:civo_mechanism}). \citet{tallec2019making} provide theoretical grounding via Q-function degradation under temporal resolution mismatches. All of the above aim to \emph{prevent} overestimation; our online detector complements prevention with early diagnosis during training.

\paragraph{Model Fidelity and Compounding Error.}
\citet{lambert2020objective} formalize objective mismatch; \citet{asadi2019combating} and \citet{lambert2022investigating} characterize compounding error in multi-step rollouts. \citet{ma2024harmonydream} address this in DreamerV3 by harmonizing loss weights. Our gradient decomposition confirms objective mismatch as the driver: the observation decoder channel (16--22\% of gradient) carries the $\dt$-specific spatial information that drives the fidelity gap, while the reward head (38--49\%) does not encode $\dt$-specific structure.

\paragraph{Diagnostic Methodology.}
The primacy bias \citep{nikishin2022primacy} and progressive capacity loss \citep{lyle2022capacity, nikishin2023plasticity} provide our framework for seed-dependent CIVO outcomes (\S\ref{sec:seed_divergence}). Our factorial diagnostic approach follows \citet{henderson2018deep} and \citet{andrychowicz2021whatmatters} in systematically isolating design-choice effects, extending this tradition to model-based temporal conditioning.

