\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

The results establish three insights that frame CIVO as a structural vulnerability of flat temporal conditioning in imagination-based RL. Full mechanistic analysis, gradient decomposition, and seed-divergence discussion are in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_analysis}.

\paragraph{CIVO mechanism.}
The RSSM reward head develops a $2.78\times$ prediction gap between $\dt{=}1$ and $\dt{=}2$ under trained conditioning (Table~\ref{tab:rssm_fidelity}); the critic internalizes this bias and the actor exploits it. This creates a four-stage feedback loop analogous to delusional bias \citep{lu2018nondelusional} but model-induced rather than policy-class-induced.  Full-pathway SGS (exp\_018) targets the gradient channel that maintains this bias, yet yields mixed results (50\% recovery rate). This indicates that CIVO involves mechanisms beyond gradient-mediated co-adaptation of the temporal embedding.

\paragraph{Gradient decomposition and intervention design.}
\label{sec:gradient_evolution_main}
Gradient decomposition (Figure~\ref{fig:gradient_decomp}, Table~\ref{tab:gradient_decomposition}) reveals a non-obvious structure: the reward head provides the largest gradient contribution to $e_{\Delta t}$ (49.0\% at 55K, 38.1\% at 112K), yet reward-only SGS (exp\_016e) is functionally insufficient (NVE $10.42\times$, collapse).  The observation decoder channel contributes the smallest share (16.3\% at 55K, 21.5\% at 112K) but encodes the critical $\dt$-specific spatial predictions that generate the RSSM fidelity gap.  Full-pathway SGS, which removes observation decoder \emph{and} reward gradients from $e_{\Delta t}$, \sgsclaim{} (seed~1: peak NVE $\sgsnve{}\times$, eval \sgseval{}; seed~2: collapsed, eval 40.8).  The 50\% recovery rate confirms that the observation decoder gradient pathway is causally involved but reveals that CIVO's feedback loop has redundant mechanisms not fully captured by gradient decomposition.

\paragraph{Why does $\dt$-conditioning succeed in MPC but fail in imagination-based actor-critic?}
TAWM \citep{nhu2025tawm} demonstrates that $\dt$-conditioning can be highly effective within the TD-MPC2 framework \citep{hansen2024tdmpc2}.  By conditioning the world model on $\dt$, TAWM achieves variable-rate planning that adapts temporal resolution to task demands and delivers state-of-the-art performance without value overestimation.  This success might appear to contradict our finding that $\dt$-conditioning induces CIVO.  The discrepancy is fully explained by the architectural difference between MPC and imagination-based actor-critic methods.

TD-MPC2 is an MPC architecture that optimizes action sequences directly on model rollouts via the cross-entropy method, without training a value function on imagined trajectories.  When $\dt > 1$, the world model inevitably loses some predictive fidelity, but this error remains bounded because the agent re-plans from real observed states at every decision point.  Model errors at larger temporal strides are corrected before they can compound, and no value function is trained on the imprecise predictions.  There is therefore no channel through which systematic bias can be internalized and amplified.

In DreamerV3, the architecture creates a closed feedback cycle: the RSSM generates imagined trajectories, the critic estimates values along these trajectories, and the actor optimizes against the critic's estimates.  When $\dt > 1$, the RSSM develops a fidelity gap (evidenced by the $2.78\times$ reward prediction disparity in Table~\ref{tab:rssm_fidelity}), and the observation decoder gradient pathway encodes $\dt$-specific spatial predictions that sustain this gap.  The critic, trained on these systematically biased imagined returns, develops inflated value estimates for $\dt > 1$ states.  The actor then preferentially selects actions that reach these overvalued states, which reinforces the bias and closes the CIVO loop.  This positive feedback mechanism has no counterpart in MPC, where no critic is trained on model outputs.

The vulnerability is therefore specific to the imagination-based actor-critic architecture, not to $\dt$-conditioning itself.  MPC avoids value amplification through open-loop optimization with re-planning, which prevents model error from entering a self-reinforcing cycle.  This architectural distinction also clarifies why multiple vulnerability pathways exist within the imagination-based framework: both CIVO (Pathway~A, value-driven, entropy decreasing) and non-CIVO instability (Pathway~B, eval-driven, entropy increasing, \S\ref{sec:f6}) originate from the same closed-loop structure that MPC lacks entirely.

\paragraph{Practical monitoring.}
The online detector achieves perfect sensitivity (5/5 true positives) with a false positive rate of 2/9 (22\%).  Three of five true positives trigger at or before 65K steps (range 45--65K), while the remaining two trigger later (95--105K), overlapping with the false-positive window (85--105K).  This separation yields a practical deployment guideline where alerts before 70K steps indicate high-confidence CIVO onset, and later alerts require human inspection to distinguish genuine pathology from transient fluctuations.  The two false positive cases illustrate qualitatively different failure modes: case 009c exhibits genuine value-performance dissociation (a non-CIVO pathology where the alert retains diagnostic value), whereas case 009a reflects a transient evaluation dip from which the agent recovers to near-optimal performance (eval 966).  This distinction suggests the detector functions as a general value-health monitor rather than a CIVO-specific classifier.  The underlying signal (accelerating divergence between predicted value and realized performance) captures any sustained value-performance dissociation regardless of mechanistic origin, and the 009c case demonstrates that such dissociation is a useful early-warning signal even when the root cause is not CIVO.  The current thresholds were calibrated on fourteen runs across three environments; generalization to different reward scales and training regimes requires further validation.

