\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We present a factorial diagnostic of temporal abstraction in DreamerV3 across two continuous-control environments, identifying trained $\dt$-embedding combined with multi-rate data exposure as the causal factor behind CIVO. Every variable-rate configuration degrades relative to the baseline, with degradation scaling monotonically with $\dt$. The $\nve \geq 1.5\times$ threshold achieves 15/16 classification ($n{=}16$, AUROC${=}0.969$). The RSSM develops a $2.78\times$ reward prediction gap that drives critic overestimation through a four-stage feedback loop. The same trigger produces stochastic downstream outcomes across seeds; restricting imagination to $\dt{=}1$ does not fully prevent collapse (2/4 seeds fail via distinct pathways). A lightweight online detector identifies CIVO onset with 100\% sensitivity (5/5) and predictive detection in 2/5 cases. The contrast with TAWM's success identifies the feedback architecture as the critical factor: $\dt$-conditioning succeeds with MPC re-planning but fails under imagination-based bootstrapping.

\paragraph{Limitations.}
Our factorial analysis focuses on Walker Walk; cross-environment evidence (Cheetah Run, PickCube-v1) uses fewer seeds and configurations. Both use DreamerV3/RSSM; generalization to transformer-based world models remains open. The NVE threshold is sensitive but not perfectly specific (15/16, one non-CIVO false positive). Detector thresholds were calibrated on fourteen runs; generalization to different reward scales requires validation.

\paragraph{Future directions.}
Hierarchical temporal abstraction (THICK, Hieros) may avoid flat-conditioning failure by maintaining separate dynamics per temporal scale. The 50\% SGS recovery rate points to redundant mechanisms that future interventions must address jointly. Extending this diagnostic to transformer-based world models would clarify whether CIVO is RSSM-specific. Freezing the temporal embedding while allowing adaptive horizon selection may decouple temporal abstraction from CIVO vulnerability. Code and training logs will be released upon acceptance.

